{
  "image_name": "56dda4fabe0e00a2d658abb9278205a4.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several chip-based electrochemical device designs used to study 2D materials during charging, often while being probed by an external beam. Across the layouts, a 2D material bridges metal contacts on a Si/SiO2-type substrate, with lithium and an electrolyte added locally so a bias can drive ion insertion or other electrochemical reactions.\n\nThe main comparison is between enclosed, semi-enclosed, and open platforms, plus versions adapted for TEM. In the enclosed design, the active region is sealed under a glass cover and filled with electrolyte, allowing a testing beam to access the sample from above while lithium and gold/copper contacts complete the circuit. The semi-enclosed version uses a small PDMS reservoir and adds reference and counter electrodes, suggesting more conventional electrochemical control. A dedicated enclosed TEM chip sandwiches the specimen between top and bottom chips with an imaging window so an electron beam can pass through the reaction zone. The open platforms expose a droplet or solid-state electrolyte directly on the chip, making fabrication simpler and beam access easier; one open design includes a hole and thin membrane region specifically for TEM observation. Overall, the figure shows tradeoffs between sealing, electrochemical complexity, and compatibility with optical/electron-beam characterization.",
